#
# User-scope systemd unit for the DEV-LANE ONEX deploy agent (OMN-16939).
#
# Install (the documented control path — same as the base unit):
#   cp deploy-agent-dev.service ~/.config/systemd/user/
#   systemctl --user daemon-reload
#   systemctl --user enable --now deploy-agent-dev.service
#
# ROLLOUT ORDER (OMN-24611): put the key file on the host FIRST, then install this
# unit. The unit's EnvironmentFile= and its ExecStartPre both read
# %h/.omnibase/deploy-agent-dev-signing-key.env, which holds the one
# DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET line (the secret store's DEPLOY_AGENT_DEV_HMAC_SECRET
# entry delivered to that path). A unit installed before the file exists fails
# every start (the file is required, no leading `-`) until the file lands.
#
# WHY A SECOND UNIT EXISTS
#
# deploy-agent.service's tracked drop-in (deploy-agent.service.d/override.conf,
# OMN-15181 Finding 3) deliberately pins that process to the PROD control bus
# (192.168.86.201:49092). Its own header records the tradeoff verbatim: "this
# process cannot serve both lanes at once", with a per-lane instance named as
# the way out (OMN-13810). This IS that per-lane instance for dev.
#
# The consequence of only having the prod-pinned unit was measured on
# 2026-09-06: onex.cmd.deploy.rebuild-requested.v1 on the DEV broker had zero
# consumer groups while four correctly-formed, HMAC-signed rebuild commands sat
# on it unconsumed, so the dev lane could not track `dev` over the bus no matter
# how healthy the redeploy FSM upstream of it became.
#
# This unit does NOT touch deploy-agent.service. Prod's unit keeps its bus, its
# port (8099) and its state dir; this one takes 8098 and its own state dir, so
# the two can be enabled at the same time without a single-flight or port
# collision. The Kafka consumer group id is the same on both, which is correct:
# they are on different brokers, so the groups are different groups.
#
# LANE FENCE (required, fail-closed)
#
# DEPLOY_AGENT_ALLOWED_LANES is not decoration. The dev control bus carries
# BOTH dev and stability-test rebuild commands by design — see
# .github/workflows/runtime-rebuild-trigger.yml: "Control-bus routing is
# independent of the requested runtime lane. Both dev and stability-test
# commands enter through the dev control bus." Without the fence, a
# stability-test command landing on the dev broker would be executed by this
# agent against the stability-test lane, which is a governed lane this process
# has no business mutating. deploy_agent.lane_policy refuses to start when the
# variable is unset — there is no default.
#
# DEPLOY_AGENT_FOLLOW_STABILITY=1 (OMN-21485) adds the stability lane to a lab
# dev fence on purpose: this agent then keeps that lane on the omnimarket dev tag
# the dev lane ran with a passing deploy record
# (deploy_agent.idle_converge.decide_stability_follow), because
# refresh_stability_lane.sh is refused from every remote lane and the pinned
# release path has no tag that carries sibling staging. Prod is never added: a
# fence naming prod is a prod agent, and lane_policy ignores the setting there.
#
# CONTROL-BUS TRANSPORT (declared, never inferred — OMN-18012)
#
# The dev-lane Redpanda EXTERNAL listener on :19092 has required SASL since
# ~16:40Z on 2026-09-07 (OMN-18012 Phase B): SCRAM-SHA-256 over PLAINTEXT —
# authenticated, NOT encrypted, there is no TLS on that port. This unit
# DECLARES that transport beside the broker it already declares, exactly as
# the CI overlay lane table config/lab/ci_bus_lanes.yaml declares `security_protocol` /
# `sasl_mechanism` beside each CI lane's broker and as
# docker/docker-compose.dev-lane.yml binds the same two names onto every
# dev-lane client. deploy_agent.kafka_config refuses to start on an undeclared
# transport rather than guessing one, because the guess is what broke this
# agent: it inferred the protocol from whether credentials happened to be in
# the environment, found none, bootstrapped in the clear, and the broker
# dropped every connection (53 restarts by 2026-09-08T04:12Z) while
# onex.cmd.deploy.rebuild-requested.v1 sat unconsumed on the dev bus.
#
# KAFKA_SASL_ENV_PREFIX exists because systemd cannot expand one Environment=
# value into another. The lane's SCRAM principal lives in the operator env
# file (DEPLOY_AGENT_ENV_FILE below) as DEV_KAFKA_SASL_USERNAME /
# DEV_KAFKA_SASL_PASSWORD — the same names docker-compose.dev-lane.yml
# resolves — and compose can write `${DEV_KAFKA_SASL_USERNAME}` into
# KAFKA_SASL_USERNAME while a unit file cannot. Declaring the PREFIX keeps the
# secret in the env file instead of duplicating it into a tracked unit, and
# keeps the unprefixed KAFKA_SASL_* names free: setting those globally in the
# operator env file would hand credentials to every other process that sources
# it, which is the credential-presence inference this ticket is removing.
#
# The rest of the [Service] block is the base unit's, verbatim in intent:
# canonical repo copy for both the working directory and the interpreter (the
# OMN-13760 drift this repo already fixed once), no WatchdogSec (a rebuild is a
# minutes-long synchronous subprocess that blocks the event loop; a liveness
# watchdog cannot tell it apart from a hang), Type=simple, Restart=on-failure.

#
# TRACKING REF (declared, never inferred -- OMN-16442)
#
# DEPLOY_AGENT_TRACKING_REF names the branch this process tracks: the ref
# self_update() fetches and re-execs onto, the default git_ref of a rebuild
# command that omits one, and the sibling-repo fallback for the build-arg
# cache-bust refs. It is REQUIRED and has no default -- deploy_agent.tracking_ref
# raises rather than guessing.
#
# The value is `dev`, per the operator ruling of 2026-09-08 (firm): the deploy
# agent tracks the lane deploy branch, never `main`. Until this landed, the
# literal `origin/main` was compiled into self_update. This repo's `main` is
# release-synced -- it is advanced only by an automated fast-forward to the last
# released tag and is not a PR-merge target -- so a code clone checked out on
# `dev` is hundreds of commits ahead of it. The dev agent therefore never
# self-updated, could not pick up its own fixes (#3305, #3306, #3316), and the
# manual path was the only dev deploy path for a full night.
#
# Setting this to `dev` on THIS unit does not open a prod promotion path. Prod
# deploys a pinned, stability-proven image digest -- ModelRebuildRequested
# refuses a prod command without image_digest, and prod never rebuilds from a
# ref -- and prod image promotion is governed by the OMN-13418 grant path, not
# by which branch this agent's own code tracks.
#
# ENV TRANSPORT: NO EnvironmentFile= FOR THE OPERATOR STORE (OMN-18073)
#
# This unit deliberately declares NO EnvironmentFile= for the operator store (the one
# it does declare is the dev signing key, below). ExecStart runs
# deploy/deploy-agent-launch.sh, which bash-`source`s the operator env store
# and then execs the interpreter -- exactly what onex-runtime-deploy has
# always done.
#
# Why: systemd's env-file parser does not implement bash ANSI-C $'...' quoting.
# It keeps the literal $' / ' wrapper and drops every backslash escape, so each
# \n collapses to the bare letter n. The store carries ONEXBOT_OCC_PRIVATE_KEY
# in that form. Measured on the lab host 2026-09-09, by length and sha256-12
# only: bash `source` yields 1679 bytes that parse as a PEM; EnvironmentFile=
# yields 1682 bytes still wrapped, with zero backslashes. The agent hands
# dict(os.environ) to `docker compose`, whose interpolation wrote the mangled
# key into every dev-lane container it created -- a 100% OCC-mint outage that
# ran for eight hours, while the sanctioned script path produced correct
# containers from the same file on the same host.
#
# DEPLOY_AGENT_ENV_PROTECTED is what keeps this faithful rather than merely
# different. systemd applies directives in file order, so the Environment=
# lines that followed EnvironmentFile= used to WIN over the store; a plain
# `set -a; source` inverts that. The store defines KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS and
# KAFKA_ENVIRONMENT, and its KAFKA_ENVIRONMENT is `local` while this unit
# declares `dev` -- so without the protected list this launcher would silently
# relabel the dev agent's lane. Every name this unit declares must appear in
# that list; tests/unit/test_deploy_agent_launcher_omn18073.py derives the
# expected set from this file and fails when a new Environment= line is added
# without extending it.
#
# executor._compose_env()'s UndecodedAnsiCQuotingError guard is NOT replaced by
# this. It stays as the fail-closed backstop for any path that still arrives
# mangled -- including this unit installed without a matching daemon-reload.
#
# THE ONE EnvironmentFile= THIS UNIT DOES DECLARE (OMN-24565)
#
# The operator env store carries ONE signing key, DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET, and
# the live deploy agent reads the same name from the same file. The dev agent
# must not hold that key (and a lab host that sends a signed dev command must not
# need it), so this unit reads its own dev-only key from a second file that holds
# that one key and nothing else:
#
#   EnvironmentFile=%h/.omnibase/deploy-agent-dev-signing-key.env
#
# systemd's parser is safe for it: the file is one `DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET=<key>`
# line, and the generated key is letters, digits, `-` and `_` only (the secret store
# node refuses to write any other value), so it has no quote or backslash for the
# OMN-18073 mangling to touch. The file is NOT optional (no leading `-`): an unreadable file fails the
# start loudly, where a missing key would start an agent that rejects every
# signed command. The value is the secret store's DEPLOY_AGENT_DEV_HMAC_SECRET,
# the dev-only entry that node_secret_store_effect generates; the file is its
# delivery to this host, and a value is never written in this repository.
#
# Why that beats the store: DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET is listed in
# DEPLOY_AGENT_ENV_PROTECTED below. The launcher snapshots every protected name
# before it sources the store and restores it after, so the dev key survives the
# store's shared value; deploy_agent.executor.operator_store_overlay leaves the
# protected names out of its re-read, so no compose command re-applies the shared
# value either. The dev runtime's containers (which sign with the same name) get
# the dev key too, because compose interpolates ${DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET} from the
# agent's environment (docker-compose.infra.yml) and nothing loads the operator
# store around it: no compose or other YAML file in this repository declares an
# `env_file:` key, and the two compose argv the agent runs with `--env-file` are the
# standalone factory-floor-web recreate (a different compose file and project, whose
# env file is named by DEPLOY_AGENT_FACTORY_FLOOR_WEB_ENV_FILE) and a `config` render
# of the dev broker stack on the tracked docker/runtime-policy.env (no signing key in
# it). Every other `--env-file` in the scanned files is a flag of its own tool or a
# pre-PR slot script. The scans read this repository only: every YAML file, and every
# source line (extensionless scripts included) in the directories the agent runs code
# from and in the single scripts its source names, each `--env-file` line pinned by
# its text, in tests/unit/test_dev_agent_own_signing_key_omn24565.py. They do not read
# the overlays repository (the agent's overlay home; its `env_file` keys belong to
# host installer units and tools) or onex-runtime-deploy (a console script of
# omnimarket, not a file of this repository): those were read by hand at their main
# branches and are named in the pull request comments, not pinned here. auth.py, the
# launcher and the compose files do not change.
#
# PORT PREFLIGHT (fail-fast, never destructive)
#
# ExecStartPre runs deploy/preflight_port_free.sh, which refuses to start
# when the port is already held and names the holder (command + pid) on
# stderr. It never terminates the holder: that process may be a peer lane's
# agent or a draining rebuild, and a unit file cannot judge the blast
# radius. Exit 2 (no socket-inspection tool, bad argument, or a probe that
# failed to RUN) also refuses -- an unprovable precondition is not a
# satisfied one.
#
# A refusal must also STOP. With Restart=on-failure/RestartSec=5 and
# systemd's default start limit (5 starts in 10s), five restarts spaced 5s
# apart span ~25s and never trip it, so a held port would re-refuse every
# five seconds forever and bury the journal. StartLimitIntervalSec and
# StartLimitBurst below bound that: the unit lands in `failed` within ~30s,
# where it is visible instead of merely noisy.

[Unit]
# OMN-16939: bound the fail-fast refusal loop (see PORT PREFLIGHT above).
StartLimitIntervalSec=300
StartLimitBurst=5
Description=ONEX Deploy Agent (dev lane) — Kafka-driven rebuild orchestrator
After=network-online.target docker.service
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
WorkingDirectory=/data/omninode/omnibase_infra/scripts/deploy-agent
# OMN-24565: the dev agent's own signing key, see THE ONE EnvironmentFile= above.
EnvironmentFile=%h/.omnibase/deploy-agent-dev-signing-key.env
# OMN-19522: the agent runs a bare `uv` (self-update's `uv sync`, build steps'
# `uv run`), and uv lives in ~/.local/bin, which a systemd user manager's default
# PATH does not carry. Declared here rather than inherited from whatever the
# host's user manager happens to hold; %h is the unit user's home. PATH is in
# DEPLOY_AGENT_ENV_PROTECTED so the sourced env store cannot replace it.
Environment=PATH=%h/.local/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_ENV_FILE=/home/jonah/.omnibase/.env
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_PYTHON=/data/omninode/omnibase_infra/scripts/deploy-agent/.venv/bin/python
# OMN-20255: OMNI_HOME is the DEPLOY-SOURCE root, the tree reconcile-host converges
# (it resolves OMNI_HOME=/data/omninode from /data/omninode/omnibase_infra/.env)
# and the root its OMN-20154 lock lives under. A workspace build's RT-1 step
# (stage_workspace.sh -> deploy_source_ref.py) runs checkout --force, reset --hard
# and clean -ffdx on every vendored sibling under OMNI_HOME. The env store above
# selects the lanes' canonical tree, so before
# this line every dev job detached omnimarket and omnibase_core
# there, and the clone guard refused every in-process delegate while they stayed
# detached. Declared and protected here so the env store cannot move it back.
Environment=OMNI_HOME=/data/omninode
# OMN-20037: scheduled scripts run from the registry, not the deploy-source root.
Environment=UNIT_DRIFT_REGISTRY_ROOT=/data/omninode/omni_home
# OMN-20637: same declaration as the host operator env used by stability refresh.
Environment=DEPLOY_SOURCE_CLONE_ROOT=/data/omninode
Environment="DEPLOY_AGENT_ENV_PROTECTED=PATH OMNI_HOME UNIT_DRIFT_REGISTRY_ROOT DEPLOY_SOURCE_CLONE_ROOT DEPLOY_AGENT_ENV_FILE DEPLOY_AGENT_PYTHON DEPLOY_AGENT_ENV_PROTECTED DEPLOY_AGENT_DIR DEPLOY_AGENT_STATE_DIR DEPLOY_AGENT_REPO_DIR DEPLOY_AGENT_PORT DEPLOY_AGENT_TRACKING_REF DEPLOY_AGENT_ALLOWED_LANES DEPLOY_AGENT_FOLLOW_STABILITY ONEX_LEDGER_WRITE_VIA ONEX_LEDGER_HOST_NAME KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS KAFKA_ENVIRONMENT KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL KAFKA_SASL_MECHANISM KAFKA_SASL_ENV_PREFIX DEPLOY_AGENT_LANE_BUILDER DEPLOY_AGENT_OVERLAY_HOME_CLONE DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET"
# OMN-21279: the host agent reports a failed dev boot even while the runtime
# bus workers are down. The ledger writer is bus-only; its local ledger is a
# read mirror, not a second ledger of record. The guard retains unacknowledged
# incidents in the job state directory and retries after recovering dev.
Environment=ONEX_LEDGER_WRITE_VIA=bus
Environment=ONEX_LEDGER_HOST_NAME=h201
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_DIR=/data/omninode/omnibase_infra/scripts/deploy-agent
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_STATE_DIR=/data/omninode/deploy-agent/state/jobs-dev
# OMN-24562: the agent's OWN deploy-source clone (under its state dir), created on
# first start at the commit the agent was serving (the canonical clone gives its origin
# url only, never a commit). The agent resets and cleans only this
# path. The canonical clone it never mutates (DEPLOY_AGENT_CANONICAL_CLONE) and the
# overlay source (DEPLOY_AGENT_LAB_OVERLAY_SOURCE) are declared in the host env store.
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_REPO_DIR=/data/omninode/deploy-agent/state/jobs-dev/deploy-source/omnibase_infra
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_PORT=8098
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_TRACKING_REF=dev
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_ALLOWED_LANES=dev
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_FOLLOW_STABILITY=1
# OMN-19211: lane image builds run on the dedicated builder declared as `lane` in
# deploy/buildkit/builders.yaml, not on dockerd's embedded BuildKit.
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_LANE_BUILDER=lane
# OMN-22634: the overlay home's clone, whose origin/main holds the runtime_lane_env
# overlay the agent reads beside the deployment fragment (executor._lane_env_overlay).
# The code has no default: a unit that names no clone reads none, and a unit that names
# one the agent cannot read refuses. Protected, so the env store cannot move it.
Environment=DEPLOY_AGENT_OVERLAY_HOME_CLONE=/data/omninode/overlays
Environment=KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS=192.168.86.201:19092
Environment=KAFKA_ENVIRONMENT=dev
Environment=KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL=SASL_PLAINTEXT
Environment=KAFKA_SASL_MECHANISM=SCRAM-SHA-256
Environment=KAFKA_SASL_ENV_PREFIX=DEV_
# OMN-24611: the key FILE must hold a usable key -- refuse the start when it does not.
#
# The EnvironmentFile= above is required-to-exist (no leading `-`), but that is
# not required-to-parse. A broken file leaves DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET unset or
# empty in the environment systemd hands ExecStartPre (EnvironmentFile= applies to
# ExecStartPre), and the launcher then either keeps the EMPTY value or sources the
# operator env store:
#
#   1. an empty-valued line (`DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET=`) sets the name to the
#      EMPTY string, which the launcher's protected snapshot restores: the agent
#      starts with an empty key;
#   2. a wrong-named line (`DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC=...`) sets only the wrong name --
#      the real one is never set at all;
#   3. an unparseable line (not `NAME=value` at all) sets nothing.
#
# In cases 2 and 3 the agent silently runs on the SHARED signing key -- exactly what
# the unit's own key file exists to prevent. The launcher is a baselined file that
# cannot grow the check, so the unit refuses the start itself: exit 2, printing only
# the NAME to stderr, never a value. It is ordered BEFORE the port preflight below and
# runs before the launcher sources the store.
#
# The check reads the key FILE, not the assembled environment: when the user
# manager already carries DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET (environment.d, `systemctl --user
# import-environment`), the name is protected, so that inherited value would
# survive a broken file and a test on the environment would pass it. The file must
# hold exactly ONE line, and that line must be `DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET=<key>` whose
# key is letters, digits, `-` and `_` (the whole line, `grep -x`, so an empty,
# whitespace-only or quoted value, or trailing blanks, fails); the template
# placeholder `__REPLACE_WITH_...__` (which has only those characters) is refused by
# name. Every other line is refused -- comments and blank lines included -- because
# systemd does not read the file the way a line-anchored grep does: it strips leading
# blanks and takes the LAST assignment of a name (an indented second assignment
# wins), a comment line ending in a backslash continues onto the next line and
# swallows a key line that follows it, and any other NAME=value line (for example
# DEPLOY_AGENT_ENV_PROTECTED=) overrides the unit's own Environment=, since an
# EnvironmentFile= setting beats Environment= (systemd.exec). A file that is exactly
# the key line has none of those. `%h` is the unit user's home, expanded by systemd
# in this line exactly as in EnvironmentFile=. `$$` is systemd's escape for a literal `$`.
#
# THE EXIT-2 REFUSAL DOES NOT LOOP. systemd's RestartPreventExitStatus= tests the
# MAIN process's exit status, not a control process such as ExecStartPre, so it
# would not stop this refusal; the bound is the start limit in [Unit]. With
# Restart=on-failure and RestartSec=5, a refused start is retried at 5 s spacing
# and the sixth start inside StartLimitIntervalSec=300 is refused by systemd, so the
# unit lands in `failed` after five tries (~25 s) instead of looping forever.
ExecStartPre=/bin/sh -c 'f="%h/.omnibase/deploy-agent-dev-signing-key.env"; test "$$(grep -c "" "$$f")" = 1 && grep -Eqx "DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET=[A-Za-z0-9_-]+" "$$f" && ! grep -Eqx "DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET=__REPLACE_WITH_.*" "$$f" || { echo "deploy-agent-dev: the key file does not hold exactly one valid DEPLOY_AGENT_HMAC_SECRET line, refusing to start (OMN-24611)" >&2; exit 2; }'
ExecStartPre=/data/omninode/omnibase_infra/scripts/deploy-agent/deploy/preflight_port_free.sh 8098
ExecStart=/data/omninode/omnibase_infra/scripts/deploy-agent/deploy/deploy-agent-launch.sh
Restart=on-failure
# OMN-20410: on-failure counts a SIGTERM exit as clean. earlyoom SIGTERMed this
# agent at 2026-10-03T16:09:11Z and systemd left the dev lane with no deploy
# agent for three hours. An explicit `systemctl --user stop` still stops it.
RestartForceExitStatus=SIGTERM
RestartSec=5
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=deploy-agent-dev

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
